19,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,3862,6724,264,1931,1372,400,74,3,1741,429,279,23606,198,59,26056,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,19,24984,481,16,1124,408,90,5187,2555,92,488,259,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,20,24984,220,17,1124,408,90,5187,2555,92,284,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,23,24984,595,1124,408,90,5187,2555,92,488,274,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,481,16,20,24984,481,21,1124,408,90,5187,2555,11035,60,4648,71500,1657,9904,304,400,83,3,323,400,82,12947,220,7379,400,74,12947,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nThere exists a real number $k$ such that the equation\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 8 \\\\ k \\end{pmatrix} + s \\begin{pmatrix} -15 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}\\]has infinitely many solutions in $t$ and $s$.  Find $k$.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"cc8d3b227f1d6066b5236542df5096eaf1c4a80b5488615828b37eea1fe2d196"},"seconds":11.936808824539185,"seed":706179751919669538,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine the value of \\( k \\) such that the given vector equation has infinitely many solutions in \\( t \\) and \\( s \\), we start by equating the components of the vectors on both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 8 \\\\ k \\end{pmatrix} + s \\begin{pmatrix} -15 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis can be written as two separate equations for the \\( x \\)-components and \\( y \\)-components:\n\n1. \\( 4 + 5t = 8 - 15s \\)\n2. \\( -1 + 2t = k - 6s \\)\n\nWe can rearrange these equations to isolate the terms involving \\( t \\) and \\( s \\):\n\n1. \\( 5t + 15s = 4 \\)\n2. \\( 2t + 6s = k + 1 \\)\n\nWe notice that the first equation can be simplified by dividing every term by 5:\n\n\\[\nt + 3s = \\frac{4}{5}\n\\]\n\nThe second equation can be simplified by dividing every term by 2:\n\n\\[\nt + 3s = \\frac{k + 1}{2}\n\\]\n\nFor the system to have infinitely many solutions, the two equations must be equivalent, meaning their right-hand sides must be equal. Therefore, we set the right-hand sides equal to each other:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4}{5} = \\frac{k + 1}{2}\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\( k \\), we cross-multiply:\n\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 2 = 5 \\cdot (k + 1)\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n8 = 5k + 5\n\\]\n\nSubtract 5 from both sides:\n\n\\[\n3 = 5k\n\\]\n\nFinally, divide by 5:\n\n\\[\nk = \\frac{3}{5}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( k \\) that makes the equation have infinitely many solutions is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3}{5}}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{3}{5}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"precalculus:168"}
